NNAAMMEE
     ddppvv — stream data from stdin or multiple paths with dialog progress view

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ddppvv [options] [_b_y_t_e_s::]_l_a_b_e_l
     ddppvv [options] --mm [_b_y_t_e_s_1]:_l_a_b_e_l_1 _p_a_t_h_1 [[_b_y_t_e_s_2::]_l_a_b_e_l_2 _p_a_t_h_2 _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ddppvv provides a dialog progress view, allowing a user to see current
     throughput rate and total data transferred for one or more streams.

     The ddppvv utility has two main modes for processing input.

     The default input mode, without ‘--mm’, ddppvv reads bytes from standard in‐
     put.  A label for the data must be provided.

     The secondary input mode, with ‘--mm’, ddppvv reads multiple paths (up to 2047
     or “ARG_MAX/2-1”), sequentially.

     Data read in either mode is either thrown away (default), sent to a
     spawned instance of the program specified via ‘--xx _c_m_d’, or sent to a
     unique file specified by ‘--oo _f_i_l_e’.

     With or without ‘--mm’, progress is displayed using one of dialog(3)
     (default), dialog(1) (see ‘--DD’), or instead Xdialog(1)
     (_p_o_r_t_s_/_x_1_1_/_x_d_i_a_l_o_g) (see ‘--XX’).

     The following options are available:

     --aa _t_e_x_t       Display _t_e_x_t below the file progress indicator(s).

     --bb _b_a_c_k_t_i_t_l_e  Display _b_a_c_k_t_i_t_l_e on the backdrop, at top-left, behind the
                   dialog widget.  When using Xdialog(1) (_p_o_r_t_s_/_x_1_1_/_x_d_i_a_l_o_g),
                   this is displayed inside the window (at the top) followed
                   by a separator line.

     --DD            Do not use the default interface of dialog(3), but instead
                   spawn an instance of dialog(1).  The path to dialog(1) is
                   taken from the DIALOG environment variable or simply
                   “dialog” if unset or NULL.

     --dd            Debug mode.  Print dialog prompt data to standard out and
                   provide additional debugging on standard error.

     --hh            Produce a short syntax usage with brief option descriptions
                   and exit.  Output is produced on standard error.

     --II _f_o_r_m_a_t     Customize the multi-file format string used to update the
                   status line.  Ignored when using either ‘--DD’ or ‘--XX’ which
                   lack the ability to display the status line (containing
                   bytes/rate/thread information).  Default value is “%'10lli
                   bytes read @ %'9.1f bytes/sec. [%i/%i busy/wait]”.  This
                   format is used when handling more than one file.

     --ii _f_o_r_m_a_t     Customize the single-file format string used to update the
                   status line.  Ignored when using either ‘--DD’ or ‘--XX’ which
                   lack the ability to display the status line (containing
                   bytes/rate/thread information).  Default value is “%'10lli
                   bytes read @ %'9.1f bytes/sec.”.  This format is used when
                   handling one file.

     --kk            Keep tite.  Prevent visually distracting initializa‐
                   tion/exit routines for scripts running dialog(1) several
                   times.

     --LL _s_i_z_e       Label size.  If negative, shrink to longest label width.

     --ll            Line mode.  Read lines from input instead of bytes.

     --mm            Multi-input mode.  Instead of reading bytes from standard
                   input, read from a set of paths (one for each label).  By
                   default, each path is processed sequentially in the order
                   given.

     --NN            No overrun.  If enabled, stop reading known-length inputs
                   when input reaches stated length.

     --nn _n_u_m        Display at-most _n_u_m progress indicators per screen.  If
                   zero, display as many as possible.  If negative, only dis‐
                   play the main progress indicator.  Default is 0.  Maximum
                   value is 10.

     --oo _f_i_l_e       Output data to _f_i_l_e.  The first occurrence of ‘%s’ (if any)
                   in ‘_f_i_l_e’ will be replaced with the _l_a_b_e_l text.

     --PP _s_i_z_e       Mini-progressbar size.  If negative, don't display mini-
                   progressbars (only the large overall progress indicator is
                   shown).  If zero, auto-adjust based on number of files to
                   read.  When zero and only one file to read, defaults to -1.
                   When zero and more than one file to read, defaults to 17.

     --pp _t_e_x_t       Display _t_e_x_t above the file progress indicator(s).

     --TT            Test mode.  Simulate reading a number of bytes, divided
                   evenly across the number of files, while stepping through
                   each percent value of each file to process.  Appends “[TEST
                   MODE]” to the status line (to override, use ‘--uu _f_o_r_m_a_t’).
                   No data is actually read.

     --tt _t_i_t_l_e      Display _t_i_t_l_e atop the dialog box.  Note that if you use
                   this option at the same time as ‘--XX’ and ‘--bb _b_a_c_k_t_i_t_l_e’,
                   the _b_a_c_k_t_i_t_l_e and _t_i_t_l_e are effectively switched (see BUGS
                   section below).

     --UU _n_u_m        Update status line _n_u_m times per-second.  Default value is
                   ‘2’.  A value of ‘0’ disables status line updates.  If neg‐
                   ative, update the status line as fast as possible.  Ignored
                   when using either ‘--DD’ or ‘--XX’ which lack the ability to
                   display the status line (containing bytes/rate/thread
                   information).

     --ww            Wide mode.  Allows long _t_e_x_t arguments used with ‘--pp’ and
                   ‘--aa’ to bump the dialog width.  Prompts wider than the max‐
                   imum width will wrap unless using Xdialog(1)
                   (_p_o_r_t_s_/_x_1_1_/_x_d_i_a_l_o_g); see BUGS section below.

     --XX            Enable X11 mode by using Xdialog(1) (_p_o_r_t_s_/_x_1_1_/_x_d_i_a_l_o_g) in‐
                   stead of dialog(1) or dialog(3).

     --xx _c_m_d        Execute _c_m_d (via sh(1)) and send it data that has been
                   read.  Data is available to _c_m_d on standard input.  With
                   ‘--mm’, _c_m_d is executed once for each _p_a_t_h argument.  The
                   first occurrence of ‘%s’ (if any) in ‘_c_m_d’ will be replaced
                   with the _l_a_b_e_l text.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The following environment variables are referenced by ddppvv:

     DIALOG     Override command string used to launch dialog(1) (requires
                ‘--DD’) or Xdialog(1) (_p_o_r_t_s_/_x_1_1_/_x_d_i_a_l_o_g) (requires ‘--XX’); de‐
                fault is either ‘dialog’ (for ‘--DD’) or ‘Xdialog’ (for ‘--XX’).

     DIALOGRC   If set and non-NULL, path to ‘.dialogrc’ file.

     HOME       If ‘$DIALOGRC’ is either not set or NULL, used as a prefix to
                ‘.dialogrc’ (i.e., ‘$HOME/.dialogrc’).

     USE_COLOR  If set and NULL, disables the use of color when using
                dialog(1).  Does not apply to Xdialog(1) (_p_o_r_t_s_/_x_1_1_/_x_d_i_a_l_o_g).

DDEEPPEENNDDEENNCCIIEESS
     If using ‘--DD’, dialog(1) is required.

     If using ‘--XX’, Xdialog(1) (_p_o_r_t_s_/_x_1_1_/_x_d_i_a_l_o_g) is required.

FFIILLEESS
